content:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent dosing schedule: text says Q3W after 7 weekly doses, but Figure 1 abbreviation includes Q12W not referenced in text",
      "page": 50,
      "quote": "Q9W = every 9 weeks; Q12W = every 12 weeks; QW = every week",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inclusion criteria for Cohort F duplicate and conflict with earlier text: page 49 says HPV-positive OPSCC, but page 53 repeats same criteria with no conflict; however, page 49 states 'PD-L1 expression (CPS ≥1) is based on a prior PD-L1 result' and page 53 repeats, but no issue. Actually, the issue is that page 49 and page 53 both contain identical inclusion criteria for Cohort F, but page 49 is in the treatment section, not inclusion criteria, which is a cross-reference error.",
      "page": 49,
      "quote": "For subjects with documented history of oropharynx primary disease, either of the following will be accepted as evidence of HPV-positive OPSCC: positive p16 immunohistochemistry (IHC) or positive HPV DNA or RNA in situ hybridization (ISH).",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent contraception duration: exclusion criterion says 6 months after last dose, but text elsewhere says 150 days after last dose",
      "page": 56,
      "quote": "from the time of consent until at least 150 days after last dose of study treatment.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent treatment discontinuation criteria: Section 3.2 says treatment continues until 2 years, but Section 3.2.1 allows treatment beyond progression, and Section 3.2 says '2 years of study treatment' as a discontinuation criterion, but Section 3.2.1 says 'Treatment beyond the second progression may be allowed' which contradicts the 2-year limit? Actually, the 2-year limit is a maximum, but the text says 'up to 2 years' and also says '2 years of study treatment' as a reason to stop, which is consistent. However, the protocol says 'All subjects will receive vidutolimod and cemiplimab according to the treatment schedule up to 2 years' (page 49) and Section 3.2 says '2 years of study treatment' as a discontinuation criterion, but Section 3.2.1 allows treatment beyond progression, which could extend beyond 2 years? The text does not explicitly state that treatment beyond progression can extend beyond 2 years, but it is implied. This is a potential contradiction.",
      "page": 51,
      "quote": "Subjects with a single tumor assessment of PD (per RECIST v1.1) may continue treatment until the next tumor assessment",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent imaging schedule: text says disease assessments every 9 weeks, but Figure 1 abbreviation includes Q12W, and PTFU says every 3 months, which is not necessarily 12 weeks but could be, but the protocol does not specify if PTFU assessments are every 12 weeks or every 3 months, which is a minor inconsistency.",
      "page": 49,
      "quote": "repeated every 9 weeks (e.g. W19D1, W28D1, etc.)",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent exclusion criterion for prior PD-1 therapy: Cohort A2 and B2 require progression within 12 weeks of discontinuation, but exclusion criterion 4 says history of immune-mediated AE leading to permanent discontinuation due to prior PD1-blocking antibody, which is not necessarily a contradiction but could be a conflict if a subject had an imAE but did not progress within 12 weeks? Actually, the exclusion criterion is about imAE, not progression, so no direct contradiction.",
      "page": 55,
      "quote": "History of immune-mediated AE leading to permanent discontinuation due to prior PD1–blocking antibody.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent inclusion criterion for measurable disease: says 'at least 1 accessible lesion amenable to repeated IT injection' but also says 'A previously irradiated lesion may be used as a target lesion if subsequent disease progression in that lesion (at least 20% increase in dimensions with a 5 mm absolute increase) was documented.' This is not a contradiction but a clarification.",
      "page": 54,
      "quote": "A previously irradiated lesion may be used as a target lesion if subsequent disease progression in that lesion (at least 20% increase in dimensions with a 5 mm absolute increase) was documented.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent exclusion criterion for corticosteroids: Section 3.6 says 'Received systemic pharmacologic doses of corticosteroids > 10 mg/day prednisone within 15 days before first dose' but Section 3.8 says 'Systemic pharmacologic doses of corticosteroids > 10 mg/day of prednisone equivalent are not permitted within 15 days of study enrollment.' The timing differs: 'before first dose' vs 'within 15 days of study enrollment' – enrollment may be before first dose, so this is a potential inconsistency.",
      "page": 55,
      "quote": "Received systemic pharmacologic doses of corticosteroids > 10 mg/day prednisone within 15 days before first dose of study treatment on W1D1.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent exclusion criterion for live vaccination: Section 3.6 says 'within 30 days before first dose' but Section 3.8.1 says 'within 30 days before first dose' – consistent.",
      "page": 56,
      "quote": "Received a live virus/attenuated vaccination within 30 days before first dose of study treatment on W1D1.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent treatment discontinuation for CR: Section 3.2 says 'treatment with vidutolimod or the combination of vidutolimod and cemiplimab may be discontinued at the Investigator’s discretion once they meet both criteria' but does not specify if both drugs must be discontinued together; however, earlier it says 'Subjects who permanently discontinue treatment with cemiplimab because of immune-mediated AEs (imAEs) must also permanently discontinue treatment with vidutolimod.' This is not a contradiction but a clarification.",
      "page": 51,
      "quote": "If a subject achieves and maintains a confirmed CR by Investigator review, treatment with vidutolimod or the combination of vidutolimod and cemiplimab may be discontinued at the Investigator’s discretion",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent end of study definition: Section 3.4 says 'the date of global last subject’s last visit (100 days ±7 days after the last dose of study drug: vidutolimod or cemiplimab), or the date of withdrawal from the study, or lost to follow-up (i.e., the study subject can no longer be contacted by the investigator) whichever comes first.' But Section 3.2 says treatment continues up to 2 years, and PTFU and LTSFU are conducted every 3 months after EOT, which could extend beyond 100 days after last dose. The end of study is defined as 100 days after last dose, but LTSFU is conducted every 3 months after EOT, which would be after the end of study? This is a contradiction.",
      "page": 52,
      "quote": "The definition for End of Study (Trial) is the date of global last subject’s last visit (100 days ±7 days after the last dose of study drug: vidutolimod or cemiplimab), or the date of withdrawal from the study, or lost to follow-up (i.e., the study subject can no longer be contacted by the investigator) whichever comes first.",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent follow-up schedule: Section 3.2 says 'Long-term survival follow-up will be conducted every 3 months after the EOT visit or the last disease assessment date in PTFU.' But Section 3.4 says end of study is 100 days after last dose, so LTSFU would occur after end of study, which is contradictory.",
      "page": 51,
      "quote": "Long-term survival follow-up will be conducted every 3 months after the EOT visit or the last disease assessment date in PTFU.",
      "severity": "critical"
    }
  ]
}
